# tests/utf8/axis11_startpos_boundary.rxt — [K50] CANDIDATE MATCH STARTS ARE
# CHARACTER BOUNDARIES.
#
# THE FIRST FILE IN THIS DIRECTORY THAT IS NOT ONE OF THE D27 EXTRACT'S AXES,
# and it exists because the extract had no section for this: the blinded
# author wrote from `docs/design/utf8_d27_extract.md`, whose §2.6.1 covers a
# CALLER-supplied mid-character `startpos` and says nothing about the
# positions the ENGINE invents — because until K50 nobody knew the engine
# invented any bad ones. This directory's own CLAUDE.md named the gap after
# the fact: *"This corpus has no `\B`-under-`utf8` cell; the natural place for
# one is a future axis."* This is that axis.
#
# WHAT IT PINS. Under `-e utf8` an unanchored search's candidate match starts
# are exactly the encoding's character boundaries, so no reported span begins
# inside a multi-byte character. That was FALSE until 2026-09-06: the DFA's
# start-anywhere self-loop stepped one BYTE (`nfa_wrap_unanchored`) and
# `ENG_ATTEMPT`'s attempt loop stepped one byte (`start++`), so a pattern that
# can match EMPTY answered at mid-character offsets.
#
# WHY THE FAMILY IS EXACTLY THIS SHAPE, which is the part to keep when adding
# to the file. A mid-character start is only reachable as an ANSWER for a
# pattern that can match empty there: under UTF-8 no lowered pattern begins by
# consuming a continuation byte, so a positive pattern simply finds nothing
# and the wasted attempt is invisible. `utf8_design.md` §2.6.1's inversion is
# what makes the empty case different — a NEGATIVE assertion succeeds exactly
# where its body has no path — and `\B` is the same shape without needing an
# assertion body at all. A cell built from a positive pattern cannot detect
# any of this, which is why there are none here.
#
# ORACLE: libpcre2 10.46 under `PCRE2_UTF`, the project's reference build, run
# over ssh and archived at `docs/design/utf8_measurements/out/startbnd.txt` §1
# — every expectation below is a row of that transcript rather than a
# derivation. `# pcre2-only` throughout, for this directory's own standing
# reason (its CLAUDE.md: `verify_rxt.py`'s subject decoder is byte-oriented,
# so a multi-byte subject is the wrong number of characters under python).
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, and where it is instead. The CALLER-startpos half of K50 —
# the default-on boundary guard and its `-fno-startpos-guard` arm — cannot be
# written as `.rxt` cells today: a refusal at a NON-ZERO startpos has no
# directive (`gu` is offset-0 only) and no directive spells the flag. Both are
# `.rxt`-format questions rather than this lane's to invent, so that half is
# carried by `tests/utf8/run_startbnd_diff.sh`, which sweeps both arms over
# every startpos of every subject. See that script's header.

# pcre2-only
# K50's OWN WITNESS, restored from tests/known_fail/ where it was filed on
# 2026-09-05 and held loud until the DFA half was fixed. `61 CE B1` is ASCII
# `a` then alpha; the character boundaries are 0, 1 and 3, and byte 2 is
# alpha's continuation byte. pcrec reported (2,2) — which is precisely
# libpcre2's `options=0` BYTE answer, i.e. byte semantics from a UTF-8 build.
#
# `\B` is false at 0 (start-of-subject beside the word character `a` IS a
# boundary), false at 1 (`a` then alpha crosses word/non-word) and true at 3
# (alpha precedes, end of subject follows, neither is a word character).
pattern \B
encoding utf8
features assertions
m "a\xce\xb1" 3 3

# pcre2-only
# The same defect with a consuming tail behind the assertion, which is what
# says the fix is about where an attempt BEGINS rather than about `\B`'s own
# emission: `x?` matches empty, so the reported span is unchanged.
pattern \Bx?
encoding utf8
features assertions
m "a\xce\xb1" 3 3

# pcre2-only
# THE REPORTED POSITION MOVES FURTHER INSIDE THE CHARACTER AS THE ENCODING
# WIDENS, so a three-byte subject has two wrong answers available and a
# four-byte one has three. A fix that happened to skip only ONE byte would
# pass the two-byte cells above and fail here.
pattern \B
encoding utf8
features assertions
m "a\xe4\xb8\xad" 4 4

# pcre2-only
pattern \B
encoding utf8
features assertions
m "a\xf0\x9f\x98\x80" 5 5

# pcre2-only
# SITE 2, AND THE CHARTER HAD NO WITNESS FOR IT. `ENG_ATTEMPT`'s `start++`
# loop is a SECOND "try the next start" mechanism, in the emitter rather than
# the automaton, and `utf8_design.md` §5.5 plus Frank's ASK 5 ruling both
# treat it as merely wasteful. Measured wrong on 2026-09-06: this pattern at
# `startpos = 1` (a REAL character boundary) reported (2,2).
#
# THE PATTERN IS TWO BRANCHES ON PURPOSE AND NEITHER IS OPTIONAL. The
# BOT-family branch is what routes the pattern to `ENG_ATTEMPT` at all; the
# nullable second branch is what keeps an interior start state live, so
# `start_max` is the subject length and the loop runs past its first
# iteration. A pure `(?m)^` pattern is SELF-GATING — `(?m)^` can only hold
# after a newline and a newline is a character-start byte — which is why this
# site went unwitnessed for a whole milestone.
pattern (?m)^a|\B
encoding utf8
features assertions,modifiers
ms 1 "a\xce\xb1" 3 3

# pcre2-only
# The same pattern's other startpos values, so the cell above is a fact about
# position 1 rather than about the pattern. At 0 the `(?m)^a` branch answers
# immediately; at 3 (end of subject) `\B` holds.
pattern (?m)^a|\B
encoding utf8
features assertions,modifiers
ms 0 "a\xce\xb1" 0 1
ms 3 "a\xce\xb1" 3 3

# pcre2-only
# ILL-FORMED BYTES STAY STARTABLE — `utf8_design.md` §2.6(c), the rule the fix
# had to preserve while gating everything else. 0xFF is not a continuation
# byte, so offset 1 of `61 FF` IS a character boundary and a search must still
# find a match there. A fix that gated on "is a valid LEAD byte" instead of
# "is not a CONTINUATION byte" would pass every cell above and fail this one.
pattern a
encoding utf8
m "\xffa" 1 2
